}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ef8185-3b01-5cc2-a040-a6ce07a57c3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396284fb-66e1-5043-b407-7357db7c21ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "396284fb-66e1-5043-b407-7357db7c21ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523036} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4eda86-0324-54cf-b300-9a498fb8794e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da8bb941-6503-5014-b798-77c51b79d5e5" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+11,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-6\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d337060-1e09-500e-a725-817b12e948cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523023} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24db76-c425-5c85-8ebb-d6b5749020ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523040} {:answer/id #uuid "a7936598-9d1f-55c4-9dd9-7abc95040343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523060} {:answer/id #uuid "bb44dca9-460c-5f8e-a9be-d5ddfc0acf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523068} {:answer/id #uuid "c509673f-074a-50e2-8f60-18ed364b8a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c509673f-074a-50e2-8f60-18ed364b8a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523074} :answer-field/id #uuid "afcde732-d4fd-5d99-bec4-a5da89e670fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee8bc31a-d21a-5520-b1c9-d397836ee8dd" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{5t},\\,y=4{t}^{2}-8t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{e}^{5t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t-8=0\\,⟹\\,t=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=5{e}^{5}=5 e^{5}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}]}
